| File Path | Action | Component / Section | Change Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `src/components/desktop/Desktop.vue` | Modify | `<template>` → `<dockview-vue>` | Added `[&_.dv-content-container]:[container-type:inline-size]` so every dockview panel content wrapper becomes a size container (Tailwind `@container` equivalent), enabling `@sm:` / `@lg:` variants inside panel components. |
| `src/components/desktop/Desktop.vue` | Modify | `<template>` comment | Documented **why** the arbitrary variant exists (dockview-owned DOM node, not reachable from the template) to prevent it being "cleaned up" later. |

---

## 4. Implementation Notes & How to Use It

**What you can now write inside any panel component**

```vue
<div class="p-4 @container">
  <!-- 1 column when the panel is narrow, 2 when it passes ~28rem, 3 at ~40rem -->
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 @md:grid-cols-2 @lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
    <div v-for="item in items" :key="item.id" class="card bg-base-200 p-3">
      {{ item.name }}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Because the query container is now the **panel**, dragging the dockview sash changes the layout instantly — the exact behaviour you want for a desktop-first, multi-panel workspace (viewport queries would be useless once a panel is half-screen).

**Important clarification (Tailwind v4 semantics)**

| Class | Emitted CSS | Enables `@sm:` / `@lg:`? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `container` | `width: 100%` + max-widths per breakpoint | ❌ No |
| `@container` | `container-type: inline-size` | ✅ Yes |
| `[container-type:inline-size]` (what we added) | `container-type: inline-size` | ✅ Yes |

So the literal `container` class would **not** have enabled container queries — the panel wrappers now get the functionally correct declaration.

**Anti-patterns to avoid**
- ❌ Re-declaring `@container` on deep children of a panel "for safety" — it resets the eligible container and your `@lg:` will measure the wrong (smaller) box.
- ❌ Mixing viewport variants (`sm:`, `lg:`) and container variants (`@sm:`, `@lg:`) on the same element for the same intent — pick one per breakpoint rule so the behaviour stays predictable when panels are resized.
- ❌ Adding a `<style>` block or hard-coded px media queries for panel internals; that bypasses the container system and breaks the design-system guardrails.

**Accessibility / QA checklist**
- [ ] Resize a panel down to ~360px: content must not overflow horizontally (`@sm:` layouts collapse correctly).
- [ ] Restore a saved layout (project switch) — restored panels keep the behaviour, since the rule is on the dockview root, not per panel instance.
- [ ] Verify the dockview sash hover/drag grip (`main.css` → `.dv-sash::after`) still renders above panel content (`contain: layout` creates a stacking context only *inside* the panel).
- [ ] Confirm popovers/menus inside panels (Reka UI teleports to `<body>`) still float above the workspace.

**Scope note**
Only the main desktop dockview is affected. If vibe-coding panels (`VibeDesktop.vue`) or the file browser dockview (`FileExplorerPanel.vue`) also need panel-relative container queries, the same utility belongs on their `dockview-vue` / root element — say the word and I’ll extend it there.

